Conjoint&lt;br&gt;
Problem


How MLB front offices implicitly run&lt;br&gt;
&lt;span class="hl"&gt;Hierarchical Bayesian conjoint analysis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
every single offseason — without knowing it.


Ben Weiss · Rady School of Management · 2025




Two ways to see the same player


&lt;div style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.58em;color:var(--gold);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:10px;"&gt;📋 SCOUTING REPORT · JUAN SOTO&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:6px;font-size:.72em;"&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Contact Rate&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hl"&gt;Elite (97th pctile)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Power (ISO)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hl"&gt;Elite (.247 career)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Defense&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color:var(--slate);"&gt;Average (+2 DRS)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Age&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hg"&gt;26 — peak entering&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Contract Ask&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hr"&gt;$700M–$800M&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:.58em;color:var(--blue);letter-spacing:2px;margin-bottom:10px;"&gt;📊 CONJOINT CHOICE SET&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:6px;font-size:.72em;"&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Attribute A&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hb"&gt;Contact → Level 5&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Attribute B&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hb"&gt;Power → Level 5&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Attribute C&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="color:var(--slate);"&gt;Defense → Level 3&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Attribute D&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hg"&gt;Age → Level 5 (young)&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dim"&gt;Price&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="hr"&gt;$765M / 15 yr&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


SAME INFORMATION · DIFFERENT FRAMEWORK

Aggregate Logit vs. Hierarchical Bayes

The Core Statistical Argument



WAR is an aggregate logit model — one set of weights, applied to every team. HB estimates individual-level utilities per respondent. In a market of 30 buyers, individual-level is the entire game.




📊 Aggregate WAR Model


One fixed set of attribute weights.&lt;br&gt;
Applied identically to all 30 teams.&lt;br&gt;
A 72-win rebuilder and a 95-win contender get the &lt;span class="hl"&gt;exact same player valuation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
This is aggregate logit. It describes&lt;br&gt;no individual team accurately.




🧠 Hierarchical Bayes


Each GM = one respondent.&lt;br&gt;
Each transaction = one observed choice.&lt;br&gt;
Part-worths estimated &lt;span class="hg"&gt;per team,&lt;/span&gt; informed by but not forced to the league average.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Tampa Bay's weights look nothing&lt;br&gt;like New York's. HB captures both.




⚠️ What Gets Lost


A rebuilder values &lt;span class="hl"&gt;youth over production.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
A contender values &lt;span class="hl"&gt;proven over upside.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
A small-market team values &lt;span class="hl"&gt;OBP over power.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Averaging across teams destroys&lt;br&gt;the signal that matters most.





Analogy from class: Aggregate logit gives you the “average customer.” HB gives you every customer individually. For a 30-team market, the average is nearly useless.


Why HB works here: We have rich data per team (decades of transactions), a natural hierarchy (teams within the league), and clear heterogeneity in behavior. Textbook HB conditions.

Same Player, Four Price Tags

Individual-Level Part-Worth Utilities · HB Output in Action



Juan Soto free agency 2024 — reported offers varied by over $200M despite every team having identical public statistics. HB explains why.




⚾ DODGERS


~$700M


&lt;span class="hl"&gt;Top weight:&lt;/span&gt; Championship probability multiplier&lt;br&gt;
Soto as final piece of dynasty window&lt;br&gt;
Star power coefficient: &lt;span class="hl"&gt;+1.4×&lt;/span&gt;




WON THE BID


⚾ METS


$765M


&lt;span class="hg"&gt;Top weight:&lt;/span&gt; Fanbase re-engagement value&lt;br&gt;
Revenue recovery post-rebuild era&lt;br&gt;
Off-field brand premium: &lt;span class="hg"&gt;+$80M&lt;/span&gt;




⚾ RED SOX


~$600M


&lt;span class="dim"&gt;Top weight:&lt;/span&gt; On-field WAR production only&lt;br&gt;
Conservative long-term injury discount&lt;br&gt;
Risk penalty applied: &lt;span class="dim"&gt;−$100M&lt;/span&gt;




⚾ YANKEES


~$560M


&lt;span class="dim"&gt;Top weight:&lt;/span&gt; Short contention window only&lt;br&gt;
Core roster already locked long-term&lt;br&gt;
Length discount: &lt;span class="hr"&gt;−$140M&lt;/span&gt;




Same WAR. Same Statcast data. Same public scouting reports. Four completely different utility functions. That is Hierarchical Bayes in action — individual-level part-worths, not a league-average answer.

